To generate a table of contents (TOC) automatically in a LaTeX document, you can use the following example:

```latex
\documentclass{book} % or \documentclass{report}

% Add a blank line before this loads if not already.
\usepackage{tocloft} % optional, but provides additional customization options

\begin{document}

\tableofcontents

\clearpage % or \newpage or \chapter*

% Example content
\chapter{Chapter One}
\label{sec:firstchapter}
Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet

\chapter{Appendix}
\appendix
\chapter{Revised Terminology}
\label{sec:revisedterminology}
Redesigned Terminology

% Add the following in the document preamble (after the \documentclass line) if using with \label:
\makeatletter
\renewcommand*{\addcontentsline}[3]{%
  \iftocdepth@toc@chapter\addtocontents{toc}{\protect\contentsline{#1}{\protect\numberline{#2}{#3}}\protect\expandafter@\expandafter\z@\fi}
  \ifnum #2>\@mhooknum #3\expandafter\@gobbletwo\else\expandafter\@firstoftwo\fi
  {\ifx\nfss@\undefined\expandafter\@gobble\else\expandafter\@firstacquire\fi}
  {}
}
\makeatother

% Result (will be updated if document is modified):

% (Example document: Start with the following content; remove any printer-friendly formatting as needed)

\chapter{Introduction}
First section

\section{Second section}
Lorem ipsum

\chapter{Chapter One}
\label{sec:firstchapter}
Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet

\appendix

\chapter{Revised Terminology}
\label{sec:revisedterminology}
New terminology

\tableofcontents

% The resulting table of contents for this example document may look like this:

% (Note: The formatting of the table of contents can be adjusted using packages like tocloft.)

Contents
========

Chapter 1: Introduction ............... 1
Chapter One .......................... 2
  Revised Terminology ............... A.1

The first chapter of the document serves as an introduction and does not need a page number according to this example. The second section is an ordinary section within this document and is numbered as "2". The chapter "Chapter One" is a section within this document and is numbered as "1". The "Revised Terminology" section is an ordinary section within the "appendix" environment and therefore doesn't get a page number according to this example. Note the use of the \label commands to label the chapters and section for cross-referencing within the table of contents. Additionally, the document class can be replaced with report to achieve the same result.

